update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('07fef24a-0e0c-44d2-988b-27495e35837f'::uuid,'121c31bdfb800d29588defc5c7890915','A is about "Most boulders" moved by glaciers, so this boulder can be one of the exceptions. The argument already says it was "probably" carried a long way. At most A nibbles at the "hundreds of miles" detail. It never suggests a glacier was not the carrier, which is the claim we need to attack.'),
('6079fb88-967a-43d6-9e31-c83ae7d15037'::uuid,'29a49be2af76bcb68c6b910e0487293e','A source 50 miles to the south sounds like a handy alternative birthplace. But a southward-moving glacier could not carry rock north, and B offers no other way to move it. The "closest" source being south also leaves open a farther source to the north, which is all the glacier story needs.'),
('c4a41bc9-b580-43ac-afce-01c732e86cc3'::uuid,'561e41eb1b39f702d6d1c37e3282f0a7','This is the trap that looks like it fights the "hundreds of miles" detail. It doesn''t. The closest source being 50 miles north does not mean the boulder came from that one; there can be other sources farther north. And a northern source is exactly what a southward glacier needs, so C fits the explanation rather than hurting it.'),
('e97313c9-6429-4e0a-b101-fdd6098e6e02'::uuid,'96a139effbadcebb690e863d27ead77e','The glacier explanation only works if the boulder started north of here, because the glaciers were moving south. D says there are no volcanic sources north of the boulder at all. Then the glacier had nowhere to pick the boulder up, and the author''s explanation loses the one thing it needed.

This is the move to repeat: when an argument explains a fact with a mechanism, ask what the mechanism requires, then look for the answer that removes that requirement.'),
('eb8e8aab-45e1-4cf2-b2e4-03dca68a55a9'::uuid,'5fcd8662636c3189486ff16648266b8a','Whether other volcanic boulders sit nearby tells us nothing about how this one arrived. A glacier can drop a single boulder, so the absence of others within 50 miles is consistent with the glacier story. E would need to rule out the glacier or the northern source to weaken.'),
('35265abe-a620-4ba4-ac53-881bab2d7ae7'::uuid,'0d00cf7fed4a09880b4e188f376770fd','A gives an earlier case where more and stronger earthquakes came with a smaller radius. That is another data point showing the geologist''s link in action, so if anything it strengthens. A weakener needs earthquakes failing to shrink the radius, not succeeding again.'),
('30f37111-d65c-4381-92c1-e5bdabace383'::uuid,'be0aa9aa554b584346c55389b6b86b8c','The geologist''s chain needs the inward plate movement to leave Earth with a smaller radius. B says that during the past decade "other geological events have counteracted the movements" that follow earthquakes. If the movement was cancelled, the radius need not have shrunk, and the earthquakes no longer explain the faster spin.

The reusable move: a cause can only explain an effect if it actually operated. An answer that cancels the cause''s effect cuts the explanation at its middle link.'),
('69fbcbba-ec6a-4a2c-9c49-13936db671d9'::uuid,'23d6c0890765fe6136f77efede96c47b','The skater is only an illustration of a law of physics. Whether only skillful skaters can pull it off is about technique, not about whether a smaller radius produces faster spin. Earth does not need skill to obey the law, so C leaves the argument untouched.'),
('4af466bd-7891-4bec-ac69-7d794b98a2f6'::uuid,'e813803f39446358bee88ba4ac7dcd07','Knowing that the rotation rate has fluctuated for thousands of years does not tell us what caused this decade''s increase. Past fluctuations could have had any cause, including changes in radius. To weaken, D would need to give a different cause for this specific change.'),
('4ed90aec-1a55-4bc7-b926-12de0fb40ada'::uuid,'1854569e814e0b2f633599eb510e8736','E says plate movement increased overall, but it does not say in which direction. More movement is consistent with more of the inward movement the geologist describes. Without a claim that the radius grew or held steady, E does not undercut the explanation.'),
('35313d0b-d48d-465b-acf9-a2e5185d08d1'::uuid,'8a78818774b46772dfab8b49a4486118','Computers helping to design industrial machinery tells us computers are useful. It says nothing about which intellectual skills society values most, or whether those skills are losing value. The leap from computation to "our most important intellectual skills" is untouched.'),
('ac7199da-4955-4229-b57d-d0631874b88e'::uuid,'90ef578b87dc1991ea68765d555a26f2','B says computation time was already shrinking before computers. That does not show computation kept its value, and it does not separate computation from the skills that matter most. If anything, it is just a longer history of computation getting easier, which leaves the conclusion standing.'),
('e933847f-0634-46d0-a149-805e9410234b'::uuid,'5fe375da2ff86a4e0043dd9e359bedb2','When mathematicians and craftspeople hit their most productive years has nothing to do with how much society values their skills. The argument is about value conferred by society, and C changes no part of the link between computers and that value.'),
('3ac71c80-1166-4628-9d88-c266bfb4759b'::uuid,'24b0ad2276037481baefefc9fe26f67e','The evidence is about computations. The conclusion is about "our most important intellectual skills." The argument needs computation to be among those skills. D says the skills society values most "are not computational ones," so computers taking over computation leaves the most valued skills untouched.

This is the move: when the evidence talks about one thing and the conclusion about a bigger or different thing, look for the answer that pries the two apart.'),
('98263328-ec6d-452f-9ece-55d59bd4b70c'::uuid,'fbf754d96d82491cd116b47ac85a9857','Computers letting people do brand-new tasks is a fact about what the technology adds. It does not tell us whether the important intellectual skills keep or lose their value, and it does not say those skills are non-computational. The argument''s gap remains open.'),
('fecb34aa-1af3-4979-9429-e60201e67705'::uuid,'4fce2702aa2c8d5925ba7a0c28c93263','The argument treats three sketches as a reliable picture of the finished statue. A says Barajas''s ideas "were revised substantially throughout a series of dozens of preliminary sketches." Three sketches are then an early draft, not the final design, and the statue Barajas would have finished could look very different.

The move to repeat: find the piece of evidence the conclusion leans on, then look for the answer that shows it is not a reliable guide.'),
('2dcb5fab-5cbb-46b0-a7e0-9734b2b44113'::uuid,'de8b1fe58eedc85e4a1fb676e73b48bf','One assistant who helped plan Sonora did not sculpt it. We don''t know how many other assistants planned it, and the sculptors still had Barajas''s own sketches. Losing one person might matter a little or not at all, so B gives no clear reason the statue looks different.'),
('3ecafb41-601b-4759-978d-2ed2ffd2e866'::uuid,'20706884d8185d9bab1e3354b373f78e','The choice is hedged twice: Sonora is made "partly" of materials Barajas did not "frequently" use. So Barajas may have used them at times, and they may be only a small part of the statue. Unusual materials also need not change how the statue looks. The effect on resemblance is too uncertain to weaken.'),
('822ff3ea-9fe8-4799-a549-7a2d9dd63a14'::uuid,'0d6fc6ad5535c9a119b6b5e697533ece','If Barajas "always worked from sketches," the assistants were following Barajas''s own process. That makes the sketches look like a better guide, which strengthens the argument rather than weakening it.'),
('aaa7c821-6de5-403c-95e5-21502a96266c'::uuid,'7352a121ec8f2c3e8cdd2664dab3e0e2','Taking longer than Barajas would have says nothing about how the statue looks. Slower work could just mean careful work. The conclusion is about resemblance, so a difference in speed does not touch it.'),
('4c5033ed-c8b2-4d5d-94f5-7032144b9d07'::uuid,'1479e48b1f238ac4664f09be31815f6b','Diversifying investments across industries is a separate goal. The country could need diversification and still benefit from keeping overseas capital in place. A gives no reason the proposed laws would fail or backfire, so the recommendation survives.'),
('1e6f0245-6555-4164-b894-ac48aa65573c'::uuid,'6d1fd6f8b80e91d71299df4b057fa5f8','The economist wants "as much capital as possible" from overseas investors. B says these laws "would strongly discourage them from investing any additional capital." The laws might stop some money from leaving, but they would choke off new money coming in. The plan works against its own goal.

This is the standard way to attack a recommendation: show it backfires on the very thing it was meant to protect.'),
('6a105f40-c9ee-40bf-88f9-4a535d754380'::uuid,'bf42bfa013a8b7854fdc7aceadd6838e','Growth was highest when overseas capital was highest. That supports the premise that the country needs overseas capital, which the argument already grants. Confirming the premise gives no reason to doubt the laws, so if anything C strengthens.'),
('9338b4bc-cac8-4874-914c-046223e88a89'::uuid,'fbab6863de82c70f7c37035991be583a','D is the tempting one, because it suggests the laws are not fully effective. But "not entirely prevented" leaves room for the laws to stop most capital from leaving, which would still help the economist. To weaken a plan, we want it to fail or backfire, and D shows neither.'),
('345afe31-9399-4049-b8a4-8c5e4deeca6a'::uuid,'425cb851f17fd2ff31e3b96bd3cb2f9d','The country passed some restrictions two years ago, but E tells us nothing about what happened next. If they worked, the argument is helped; if they backfired, it is hurt. With no outcome, E has no clear effect.'),
('540e752c-aa3a-4352-868d-664d1cc15d22'::uuid,'cd1c9966a612c8c705ca6b66c82a2e85','Microscopic organisms breaking down the stored organic matter is a detail of how the nutrients might reach the orchid. That fills in the author''s own explanation rather than replacing it. We need a different reason the orchids are healthier.'),
('8620ce62-64f6-443d-8150-799dadd8b047'::uuid,'76d7e911cc1442a36b2ffd0d66ffc084','B is tempting because it makes the nutrient story look shakier: the stored matter has the same nutrients as the soil. But the stem asks for an alternative explanation, and B gives no other reason ant-housing orchids are healthier. Casting doubt on one explanation is not offering another.'),
('7682f35c-a461-48ce-816d-b08cb8b4790a'::uuid,'0cdf91302245c603629b89977a547fda','C keeps the ants as the cause but changes how they help. The ants "prey on an insect species whose members eat the orchids’ roots." Orchids with ants lose fewer roots to those insects, so they are healthier for a reason that has nothing to do with stored nutrients. That is a full second explanation for the same fact.

The move to repeat: on alternate-explanation questions, look for a different path from the same two facts, here the ants and the orchids'' health.'),
('a12f6dcb-5618-4d40-8ad2-335af2c5ad07'::uuid,'2a4953f84c2407f965c7e822feffd84d','D tells us one thing the ants don''t do: they don''t pollinate the flowers. A thing the ants don''t do cannot explain why their orchids are healthier. D removes one possible explanation and offers nothing in its place.'),
('8ed91780-476a-491a-aaba-a1772edc189f'::uuid,'b750870d6d4cddcf5db483020e287ec9','E points the wrong way: plants that harbor insects tend to be more prone to disease and rot. That would make the ant-housing orchids'' good health more surprising, not less. It is also about "Most plant species," not this orchid. No explanation here.'),
('333af76a-9d2e-463a-89b1-af6de01cb2d9'::uuid,'e994253477cfbf7a900157ef35387c34','The conclusion rests on the blind comparison showing "no difference in the severity of acne" between the groups. A says the vitamin C subjects "had a history of suffering from more severe acne" than the placebo subjects. If the vitamin C group started worse and ended up equal, it improved more than the placebo group did. That is a benefit, hidden inside a result of no difference.

The reusable test for study arguments: before trusting a comparison, ask whether the two groups started out alike. If they didn''t, equal results can mean unequal effects.'),
('8746afd0-c226-4735-9ab3-3898726ee7d9'::uuid,'14bff4a517f178c4673e432ac9286875','B rules out vitamin C subjects taking extra doses on their own. That closes a loophole, but it doesn''t make the blind comparison less trustworthy, and it gives no evidence that the vitamin worked. The no-difference result among blind subjects stands as it was.'),
('b14f1e1a-4a4f-4b57-a126-4cda19b76316'::uuid,'4516a3c5685e6d8bd724186e512da04e','C describes the subjects as a whole: their acne was below the national average. The argument compares the groups inside the study, so a fact about the whole pool doesn''t change that comparison. C gives no reason to think the vitamin C group would have been worse off without the pills.'),
('011c47e1-9daa-4d67-8a2d-7bff9623600d'::uuid,'7607136305e19c07884097c1e8af9777','D is the most tempting trap. If placebo subjects got vitamin C from food, the control group was not vitamin-free. But D says only that "Some" placebo subjects ate vitamin C-rich foods, which is an ordinary diet and could just as easily describe the vitamin C group. Nothing suggests the food matched the pill''s dose or that it happened among the blind subjects. So D barely dents the comparison, while A gives direct evidence that the vitamin helped.'),
('64024504-9bfd-41b6-ba6e-b7c4ad66198d'::uuid,'001a520ec98cdfb416489d1726f37a1b','Some subjects who knew they had placebos skipped them. Those subjects are in the half that knew, which the conclusion already sets aside. And a placebo has no active ingredient, so taking it or not changes nothing about vitamin C. The blind comparison is untouched.'),
('cc81cecf-f6fe-442b-b254-530fc6c2342c'::uuid,'f9dd130f2f8359b10b5764af288a906e','Residents who never use the branches because of irregular hours lose nothing when the branches close. If anything, A gives another reason the branches are failing residents. It shows no way the single large library would serve them worse.'),
('6d21b69b-2c6f-4d17-aa28-a4bd25c33fcc'::uuid,'0d31d88b459bd5234a5e7c6b5561b02d','Complaints about the book selection at the branches back up the argument''s premise that the branches are poorly supplied. That makes a bigger, better-stocked library sound more welcome, so B strengthens rather than weakens.'),
('dd8204b9-7a09-4650-81d9-f5617f203182'::uuid,'8acd27564c220548d8da6cc771342dd9','The argument assumes better books means better service. C says "The only possible site for a new library is not readily accessible to most Winchester residents." A library most residents can''t easily reach does not meet their needs, however good its books are. The branches, poorly stocked as they are, at least sit nearby.

The move to repeat: when an argument recommends a plan for one benefit, look for the cost it ignored.'),
('625b0672-92bf-45a7-8d48-e04bb352d8c3'::uuid,'87761a46c8aa1d291620bf3bf3adb433','The argument already says closing the branches would let Winchester afford the large library. A "significant amount of money" is consistent with that. D would weaken only if the cost exceeded what Winchester could afford, and it doesn''t say so.'),
('02f3b727-dcf3-4720-b920-9f0ba475f927'::uuid,'004bb7d2194e39ae606f783911b0aa62','If each branch tries to serve the whole township, the branches duplicate each other''s job, which makes one central library sound sensible. E gives no reason the residents would lose anything by consolidating, so it does not weaken.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;